Destination

Byte layouts and derivations. For what destinations are for and when
to use each type, see upstream docs/source/understanding.rst, section
"Destinations".

Vectors: test/destination/


Name

A destination name is an app name followed by zero or more aspects,
joined with a single dot.

    name = app_name [ "." aspect ]*

No component may contain a dot. This is enforced, not merely
conventional.

    RNS/Destination.py:102#app_name, RNS/Destination.py:106#aspect

A component may be empty. Nothing forbids it, and "a..b" is a name RNS
hashes without complaint. test/destination/empty-aspect records it,
and it is the reason the format has a spelling for the empty byte
string: hex has none, and a field name followed by nothing is a line
with a trailing space that cannot be told from a truncated one. The
empty byte string is written "-", like every other absent value.


Name hash

    name_hash = SHA-256(utf8(name))[0:10]

10 bytes, that is 80 bits, not 128. The name is hashed as its utf-8
bytes exactly as given. No unicode normalisation is applied.

test/destination/utf8-precomposed and test/destination/utf8-decomposed carry the same name in
precomposed and decomposed form. They look identical and produce
different hashes. An implementation that normalises will not
interoperate.

    RNS/Destination.py:120#expand_name


Destination hash

    destination_hash = SHA-256(name_hash || identity_hash)[0:16]

16 bytes. Without an identity, the second operand is absent:

    destination_hash = SHA-256(name_hash)[0:16]

The identity is mixed in at this step, as its 16-byte hash. It is not
part of the name that produced name_hash.

    RNS/Destination.py:130#addr_hash_material


Discrepancy with the upstream prose

docs/source/understanding.rst states that for single destinations
Reticulum "will automatically append the associated public key as a
destination aspect before hashing".

The code does not do that. Destination.hash calls expand_name with a
null identity, so the identity contributes nothing to name_hash; the
identity hash is concatenated afterwards, at the second hash. The
observable result differs from the prose, and this corpus follows the
code.

Destination.expand_name does append identity.hexhash when given an
identity, but that path produces the human-readable full name only. It
is not used in the hash derivation.

    RNS/Destination.py:109#identity and RNS/Destination.py:120#expand_name


Vector format

kind: destination, determinism: encode

raw is two lines:

    line 1    the name, utf-8 bytes, hex
    line 2    identity hash, 16 bytes, or "-" when there is no identity

The name, app name and aspect fields in expect are hex as well. A name
is arbitrary bytes: printed as text, a newline in an aspect would end
the line early and let a vector forge or hide a field. Hex also keeps
the utf-8 pair above legible, since the two names are identical as
text and differ only in their bytes.
